Z||000021||BlackStang95||11-04-2001||02:33 PM||||"So what are you saying blackstang... That a front engine car can't do wheel stands? It doesn't have anything to do with the engine in the rear dude. It's all about hp brother. See fast and the furious dude... That charger is not a rear engine vehicle and didn't have a problem lifting off the ground."<P><BR>The fiero gt has a whopping 140 hp, if its all about hp i should be able to flip a jeep cherokee over backwards.  He's not even talking about full wheelies, he was just saying lurch the front end (not tires) up off the ground, which the fiero can do due to weight in the rear.  Duh.||||reg||1
Z||000022||p4n1c||11-04-2001||02:51 PM||||Actually I can lift the frontend up too, not the tires, but when I gun my <B>130hp</B> Cutlass that weighs <B>3200lbs</B>, the front end lifts up.  And it's a front engine, front wheel drive car.  (Bolded for clarity that it has nothing to do with horsepower or weight.)<P>When stepping on the gas, the engine jolts back(and essentially downwards) in the engine bay, therefore creating an opposing force that lifts the front end up.  The more the engine slams back, the more the front end will lift up.<P>It's simple physics.  The Fiero does it more, because it has more weight in the back then the front which causes the light frontend to lift upwards from the engine rocking back.  It has nothing to do with horsepower.<P>I've had my tires come off the ground when stepping on the gas when the engine mounts were about gone.  The engine kicked so hard the whole front end lifted up(mind you for about a split second) while about to pass someone.<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International<BR>2.8L MPFI V6/TH440-T4<BR>*Under Restoration*||||reg||3
