Z||000048||Borgio||11-06-2001||12:50 AM||||Look, Mustangs don't compare to Fieros.  First of all Mustangs don't handle well compared to a Fiero. They are too tall and their center of gravity is too high, while their width is about the same as a Fiero, they are about 6inches taller. Pre '99s don't have independent suspension.<P>Looks: Mustangs would look the same as any other common two door car, except for the fake scoop on the side and the other scoop, also fake, on the hood. Newer Mustangs aren't a true sports car, as their just all hype. They aren’t unique. How many people have mustangs? Why do they have four seats?<P>They are front engine (weight isn't distributed well). Weight is irreplaceable. Fieros weight about 2700lbs, Mustangs weigh about 3500lbs. Lower weight improves handling, gas mileage, and acceleration. <P>Mustangs stock, have larger engines. They are also a larger car. They also cost more. And you are comparing new mustangs to 15 year old Fieros, which is perfectly fine, but you have to consider price. (Fieros are out of production for the same reason mustangs are in production-stupid consumers who don't know anything about cars, and enjoys the fake scoops, it’s hype,  a bad rap for Fieros, and a good early muscle car for Mustangs)<P>Price: You can have LT1 ChopTop for the $20K. Mustang GTs have little power (maybe 225hp). GTs are what most people have.  Cobras are what are fast, they cost $33K. You might as well buy a Z28 for $27K. <P>If you like body mods, even maybe some FUNCTIONAL scoops, then fieros are better. Its funny that a 15 year old fiero can easily look better thatn any Mustang. Look at 80s Mustangs now, even worse.<P>  Fieros are easier to supe up. Their parts are cheaper. Coming down to price its no contest. You not doing any work by yourself (sending it to Archie) can have an AWESOME Fiero for the price of a mediocre Mustang. There's only so much power that’s really reasonable in a street car. But you can basically put as much power on any car as any other, for the most part. Actually, I'd rather have a GM car to do it with. Both with a lot of power, a Fieros a much better one to have it on. I don’t mean to put down these types of cars, they just don’t compare to Fieros.<P>Jake<p>[This message has been edited by Borgio (edited 11-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000049||sdely||11-06-2001||01:25 AM||||I like Fieros.  The reason is because there is no car that you can get that affordably that has the performance potential.  None, period.  Fieros are well balanced, light, and posses an eerie refinement for their era. <P>Its true that in stock form they are not fireballs, but what car from the eighties is???  An 80's Mustang?  Laughable, I would blow the doors off one of those glorified Escorts no problem.  <P>Turning to the newer Stangs.  How many Mustang drivers have Cobras: very few.  Unless one has a Cobra it seems to me they have very little commitment to performance.<BR>I put a 3.1 liter engine in my car for $600, and it makes almost 200 horses.  With a turbo kit for $3000 I'm afraid you would encounter a very fast car for very little money.  This is after an initial $1000 dollar investment in the car.  Why pay $30K?  To me Stangs are more status symbols than anything.  <P>Of course when you compare a new car to one that's fifteen years old, yours will have some advantages technology wise.  That's where the advantages of the Mustang end, especially when you take price into account.<BR> <P>Personally I like to look at this as a practical issue.  For less money you can have a car that is faster, looks better, gets better gas mileage, handles better, and is much much cheaper.  But hey, why look at the practical, drive your Stangs all you want.  Its your money!  All those who would like to dwell in high dollar, fake scoop land, the Mustang is your car.  For the rest of us who want to use our creativity to propagate performance from a unique car with unlimited potential, there is the Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000050||sloth85GT||11-06-2001||02:15 AM||||Yet again, another Blackstang95 argument. Just stop it already, we all know that he's an idiot, can we just leave it at that? And we all also know that once Cliff impliments his rating system, Blackstang95 is gone. So just ignore him and stop let him ruin our good threads.||||reg||1
Z||000051||Borgio||11-06-2001||02:41 AM||||[QUOTE]<BR>This is a fiero forum...you guys should take modified versions of fieros over stock versions of mustangs, no reason to post about it.  <P>That one dudes post has too many inaccuracies for me to really even respond to.  Of course taking an old fiero and modding it will cost you less than a new mustang gt from a dealership.  From a dealership loaded fieros costed a lot, nearly 17k, which adjusted for inflation, is a lot of money.  You should compare prices evenly, or not at all.  I'm glad some of your own took it to you.  <P>1996   Chevrolet Cavalier LS                  8.3            16.1<P>1988   Pontiac Fiero GT                       8.7            16.4<P><BR>That's from a site i found, it just lists a bunch of times, they seem to run on the slow side for all of em, but damn, that should put things in perspective.  <P>btw thats the ls, not even the z24, i know my stuff.<P>Yes, a new stock Mustang is faster than a stock Fiero. Yes, a new stock Z24 Cavalier is faster than a stock Fiero. Point is that the one thing that Fieros lack is extreme power. Easily fixed. Mustangs lack many things (mentioned in my earlier post). You can get a V8 stock, but what are you gonna upgrade to, a V10? Put similar power in a Fiero, and because it’s so awesome, all around, you get a much faster, better, car. <P>Why put similar power and not compare stock?: ‘cause not all cars are stock. I know a lot of Mustangs aren’t upgraded, but Fieros are easily upgradeable for cheap, some of us have upgraded ones and those are the ones we’re wanting to compare. What we are most comparing is performance to price. Point is you can get way more out of a Fiero.<BR> <BR>I don’t care what you want to compare, use 80s Mustangs. How much did they cost? 'Oh, a V8 new Mustang is faster than a stock 1988 Fiero.' That is true, but that’s about it. Fiero is a better car for a better price. Why does anything else really matter besides performance, looks, and price? “Of course a modded Fiero will cost less than a new Mustang gt from a dealer.” Then why not do it, and have a better car? Or why not mod your 80s Mustang and have it be fast? Oh yeah, ‘cause it wouldn’t look good or handle well.<P>Jake<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000052||Borgio||11-06-2001||02:45 AM||||Sorry Sloth, I hadn't read your reply, I was too busy typing mine. I'll stop now. I read it late and wanted my voice to be heard, but understand your point.<P>------------------<BR>Jake||||reg||1
Z||000053||stimpy||11-06-2001||07:59 AM||||Where do you people get this stuff?<BR>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>With a turbo kit for $3000 I'm afraid you would encounter a very fast car for very little money. This is after an initial $1000 dollar investment in the car.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Who the hell is going to strap a turbo on to a $1000 Fiero? I'd say a $1000 V6 Fiero that hasn't been totally beat to hell and leaking oil out of several unintended orifices is far more the exception then the rule.  I propose a new rule, unless you actually made a 13 second Fiero for under $5000, then shut the hell up about it. You're talking out of your a$$ every bit as much as you say BlackStang is.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Price: You can have LT1 ChopTop for the $20K. <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>News to the owner, I'm sure. Hey Archie, will you build me a replica of the chop top for $20,000?<p>[This message has been edited by stimpy (edited 11-06-2001).]||||reg||13
