Z||000059||BlackStang95||11-06-2001||05:17 PM||||Once again, not trying to fuel the argument, just gonna correct some inaccuracies in Sporgios argument.<P>"Mustangs aren't a true sports car, as their just all hype. They aren’t unique. How many people have mustangs? Why do they have four seats?"<P>Why do people always bring up seats?   Is a miata a true sports car?  It has two seats!!!<BR>Is an old jeep wrangler?  2 seats again!  Is a fiero a true sports car?  I'm sure some of you would say yes, but it isnt in the classic sense of the word (not saying v-6 stangs are either).  All of the classic muscle cars have had 4 seats, the only one that didn't was the corvette, and even it started out as a miata kinda car (look at the 56 models).  Having 4 seats adds to the appeal of Mustangs, it doesn't detract.<P><BR>"They are front engine (weight isn't distributed well). Weight is irreplaceable. Fieros weight about 2700lbs, Mustangs weigh about 3500lbs. Lower weight improves handling, gas mileage, and acceleration. "<P>All newer Mustangs have 56/44 weight distribution, compared to 49/51 of fiero gt's.  Not a lot of difference.  Standard Mustangs weigh 3000 pounds.  Lower weight rarely improves handling...more weight pushing down on the tires makes more grip.<P>Fieros handle better than stangs for sure, but do we need to get into braking?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>"Mustangs stock, have larger engines. They are also a larger car. They also cost more"<P>No.  They don't cost any more than the fieros would today, adjusted for inflation.  The v-6 stangs are actually cheaper.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>"Mustang GTs have little power (maybe 225hp)."<P>Wrong again.  With all these simple facts wrong, how can any of your opinions be right?  265 hp (almost double what any fiero had stock...)<P>"Fieros are easier to supe up. Their parts are cheaper. Coming down to price its no contest."<P>Um....no?  You can't even find a lot of parts for them anymore.  They are fairly easy to do engine swaps in, thanks largely to the superhuman efforts of car guy Archie, but its still not easier than upgrading your existing engine or to an engine that was factory offered.<P>Moving to another post...<P>"How many Mustang drivers have Cobras?"<P>How many fiero drivers have serious swaps done?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I bet its about the same, if not more cobras.<P><BR>Mustangs as high dollar status symbols?  That'll be the day.  The corvette occupies that post, and probably always will.<P><BR>If you want to vote me off the board for correcting statements by your own idiot members (and they are a small percentage of you)  than have fun.  No skin off my nose.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000060||Mach10||11-06-2001||05:40 PM||||This is getting OLD people. For once, Blackstang95 has not said anything derogatory, rude, or propagandistic... But you guys rip into him like he has. All the bad Karma from this topic has come from us. He isn't getting MY vote off the island. Lay off. &gt;:\||||reg||13
Z||000061||Mr.Frosty||11-06-2001||05:48 PM||||I've actually checked the prices for the choptop.If you had a decent fastback,and you chopped it,then got the master build V-8 kit your looking at $6445.00 for everything (-)minus the engine.What's the going rate for a Chevy crate motor? ($3400.00)So that's just under $10,000($9845.00).Cheaper if you have a couple V-8's laying around.(like me)So figure the current value for our respective  cars.Total would be around $14,000 depending on value of the Fiero.This also doesn't include any kind of brake upgrade,but this would get it on the road.This would also require that you do the install yourself.<BR>      From Archies web site<BR>          Costs: $3250.00  V-8 master build<BR>                 $3195.00  Choptop complete with interior trim redo,and repaint of all involved surfaces.<BR> This would be with whatever rims and paint were on it to begin with.Not a bad investment considering what it could do.Now add custom paint and some nice rims you would be right around the blue choptop in price.Even taking my innaccuracies for the cost of the motor and the car into account it's still less than a 2001 Mustang GT.I know which one I'd rather have.<BR>    <P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||1
Z||000062||sdely||11-06-2001||06:00 PM||||Blackstang95<BR>  I can see your point on some of these things, and hey much of it comes down to opinion.  Like what is more important to you in a car, and pretty much what i said was oinion based.  I do however think that borgio made a good point about price.  You have to look at the today price, you don't adjust it.  You simply take what you can get.  In other words, simply how much money would it take to put me into a mustang or a fiero right now, today.  If I had to pay the adjusted price that a Fiero would be now I wouldn't have one, Thank Goodness it doesn't work that way.<BR>   Also, as I can see that you favor the Fiero GT much more than the four banger, I must point out that there is a huge varability in the kinds of Mustangs and Fieros you can get in terms of transmissions and engines which makes comparison difficult(esp. when you take mods into consideration).  <BR>   Mustangs weight for instance changes from generation to generation and with different trim packages etc.  Borgios numbers were correct just for different years etc. than you are talking about.  Plus possible mistaken info does not negate his entire argument(this is a major fallicy of logical argument).  <BR>In my post, I was simply telling the impression I have of Mustangs and I know full well that you and others may disagree, but I still have a right to my opinion and you to yours.  (In other words we just have to agree to disagree on which car is better)<BR>I still maintain that when you are on the budget I am there is just no beating a Fiero.<P>By the way, just out of curiosity, how many HP's are you pulling in your Stang? Weight?<P>Seth<BR>85 GT 3.1 4.10<P><p>[This message has been edited by sdely (edited 11-06-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000063||BN Boomer||11-06-2001||06:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by stimpy:<BR><B> First off, a $1000 Fiero is gonna need more help then just the engine. You are not gonna find one with fresh brakes, steering and suspension for a grand. And if you do, chances are it will look like a$$.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE> <P>85 GT with tired 2.8 for $1200 (Perfect candidate for engine swap.) Yeah, I know, it looks like a$$ abd all...<P> <IMG SRC="http://wsphotofews.excite.com/025/wh/x1/53/Jh29606.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://wsphotofews.excite.com/005/mD/4e/sN/Jx85839.jpg"> <P>There's also currently a low miles 3800 SC out of a 2000 Pontiac complete with cradle, tranny, harness, ECM, and halfshaftes advertised in one of the local Trader rags for $2600 OBO. Which brings us to....<P><B> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>Secondly, I find it very difficult to believe that anyone will contract to do a swap for $4000. MAYBE if you are talented enough and have the resources that Archie has, then you can bring a basic swap in at that. I mean, you are talking at least a SC3800 to have that kind of unbridaled scoot, and no-ones giving those away.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I bet I could EASILY buy and swap that 3800 SC in for under $4K. Add in another couple of grand to rebuild/upgrade the brakes, steering, and suspension, and imagine the car you could have for $7500 or so, still just a fraction of what any new or late model Mustang will set you back. I know it's over that $5K mark, but I think that is a bit on the unrealistic side, too. If one has to pay someone $10K or more for a relatively simple, straightforward engine swap and other mods, to be totally honest, they would probably be better off going out and buying a new Mustang. At least it will have some sort of warranty.<P>I look at it this way: If you had $25-30K to spend, what would you rather do, buy a stock Mustang/Camaro/TA?whatever or build an awesome Fiero? I know I'd much rather have a unique car that isn't seen very often anymore and would surprise the hell out of nearly everyone and everything.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000064||BlackStang95||11-06-2001||06:36 PM||||Of course a modded old car if you do the work yourself will run you much less than a new car.  86-89 5.0s go for 2-3k all the time, supercharger bolt ons and youre to 60 in the mid 5's.  Gotta give it some credit. Come on now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.||||reg||1
Z||000065||BN Boomer||11-06-2001||06:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BlackStang95:<BR><B>86-89 5.0s go for 2-3k all the time, supercharger bolt ons and you're to 60 in the mid 5's.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yup, still be lookin' at the tail lights of a SC3800 or V8 Fiero, too.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>Greg, aka Boomer<BR>Red 85 GT<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.gmemd.com/locomotives/naf/images/sd70sm.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000066||stimpy||11-06-2001||08:31 PM||||Wow, all of the sudden now I'm a Fiero-hater? No doubt the Fiero is one of the best hot-rodding bargains there is, but sometimes it gets a little ridiculous to see the hypothesis put forth that ANYONE can collect $5000 worth of nickles and dimes and have themselves a 13 second Fiero. I'm sure that the guys who have posted 13 second timeslips on the forum have a LOT more then $5000 in there cars. I've got more then that in mine, and I ain't nowhere near 13 seconds.<P>Now, sdely, chill pal. I never called you stupid, I just think you're conjecturing about something you have no personal experience with, i.e., talking out yer a$$. Not a mortal sin, we've all been guilty of it at one time or another. In fact, anything I say in regards to your <B>personal situation</B> is exactly that, talking out my a$$. You are right, I know nothing of the condition of your engine and the appropriateness of a turbo install on said engine. But let's examine your equation where x=cheap (high mile)Fiero, y=turbo kit, and z=new engine. Now, with x=$1000 and y=$3000, I have a hard time wrapping my mind around the math that x+y+z=&lt;$5000. Now, I suck at math, but I suck at machine work far more and I know I can't build my own engine. BUT if I could, I'd have to have a considerable amount of money wrapped up in tools and shop space. So let's hypothesize that I will have to have my engine built. In my local economy, which I'm sure is very similar to yours, a bored 2.8 with forged internals cost me $2700 installed. Now I go to Kevin Leslie at Design One and give him a check for $3200 plus tax. Again, I am at a loss for the tools and talent (as are the vast majority of consumers) and will pay a shop rate for 14-16 hours of labor. call it another $600. Already I'm in over 6 grand, without the price of my car (which admittedly, is a wide variable, but mine was $2500 for an 86 GT with 127000 miles. Better deals are out there, but it was par for the market and in good shape.)<P>So yeah, one could put a $3000+ turbo kit in a $1000 car, but unfortunately, the ENGINE is a critical variable in your equation. Does that make it a bad idea? (I won't use the word investment like you did, because if you are looking to turn a profit off of Fieros, you better be very good or very stupid. I'm neither) No, it doesn't. But it's naive to think that a terrifyingly fast, and reliable, Fiero can be built with relative pocket change.<P>(Edited cuz I really do suck at math)<p>[This message has been edited by stimpy (edited 11-06-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000067||BlackStang95||11-06-2001||09:08 PM||||Well said.  Let's let this thread die.||||reg||1
Z||000068||Borgio||11-06-2001||09:10 PM||||Mach,<P>You don't think calling us idiots is rude.<BR> <BR>Stang,<P>Just because numbers can vary doesn't mean that my numbers are wrong or "inacurate". I don't have unlimited resources or time. I was going with what I knew. <P>225  "Wrong again. Withall these simple facts wrong, how can any of your opinions be right."<BR>I'm not sure, but 225 is pretty close to 225 to me.<P>'98 Mustang GT   225hp<BR>                3400lbs<BR>These numbers are accurate.<P>Seats are just an indication that their purpose is not for sport. I was a little harsh toward Mustangs. Reason is because I thought you were being harsh toward Fieros. Not just in this post, but in others. I also don't want to give the impression that I don't like Mustangs. I do. I was thinking for price an 80s 5.0L would be nice without much work. And when I said Fieros were cheaper. I was meaning they are cheaper than the cars you are comparing them to. You're using, for the most part, very new Mustangs. And when it was said how many people have Cobras. If you remember the statement this started with, someone wanted to compare their V8 Fiero to a Mustang. I doubt his friend is going to buy a Cobra or even supe up his GT. Your right not many people have V8 fieros, but some of us do, and we're wanting to compare our cars. <P>Also lower weight does improve handling. You do get more grip, but it doesn't make up for the centrifugal force.||||reg||1
Z||000069||Mach10||11-06-2001||09:11 PM||||Agreed. Stang: You are all right. Just try ignoring some of the more flambouyant members... We'll all get along great.<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000070||sdely||11-06-2001||09:16 PM||||Well, in my case the engine was $600.  This was from a guy putting a V8 in his Fiero.  He had stroked his 2.8 to a 3.1.  <BR>Then I bought an externally balanced flywheel for $200, and a crane cam for about $100.  Also a gasket set.<BR>I got the transmission from my Uncle for free off an 84 (4spd 4.10) he had sitting.<BR>Then I put the pieces together myself. (with much help from my dad)<BR>The problem with your equation (not the equation just the numbers you entered) is you are taking it from your experience and not from my reality.  So, once I add in the price of the turbo, you do the math.  Granted not that every person could\would do this.  For some they may need to invest a couple extra thousand for labor, plus I had to hunt out a deal on the engine.<BR>I do most of the work myself which saves mucho $$$, but I guess not everyone does this.<BR>Even so, in your example you would still be building a pretty nice car for a good price.<P>Also, when I said investment I meant that the return would be reliable fun transportation for awhile, not that I would make a profit.  I put something into to it and got something out of it.<P>Also, I have no idea why you say I have NO personal experience.  Between the three Fieros I have owned I would say differently.  The only piece of conjecture was saying $3000 for the turbo, which might be a low estimate, but I would hardley characterize this as talking out of any oraface other than my mouth.<P>Seth<BR>85 GT 3.1 4.10<BR><p>[This message has been edited by sdely (edited 11-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000071||Borgio||11-06-2001||09:25 PM||||You do have to give the Mustang credit for modded mid 5sec times thats awesome. But a V8 Fiero can easily break into the upper fours. Weight is VERY important in 0-60 times. A V6 Mustang may be cheaper than a Fiero calculating in inflation. But a new Fiero would probably have more power than it does now, too. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000072||stimpy||11-06-2001||09:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sdely:<BR><B>The problem with your equation (not the equation just the numbers you entered) is you are taking it from your experience and not from my reality.  So, once I add in the price of the turbo, you do the math.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well said. However, I would imagine that my reality mirrors that of the average jamoke far more then yours does. I mean, kudos for everything you have accomplished so far, but I have also seen people put far more then the cost of a new Mustang GT into a Fiero buildup and still have some reliability issues. Not because the Fiero is an inherently bad design (blasphemy!) but because a swap is going to run into bugs. Regardless. So, when you're done doing your turbo (which I wish you the very best success) and getting it to run reliably, then total up your receipts. You may see things a little bit more realistically. <P>Peace. ||||reg||1
Z||000073||sdely||11-06-2001||09:55 PM||||Yes stimpy,<BR>I agree completely with your last post.  From what I've done so far I've already learned not to trust estimates, I mean on old cars things go wrong.  Even now I am debugging, but thats part of my love for the car.  When I am older perhaps another car will be better for me, and who knows whether my turbo dream will ever come true. I wish that everyone could have an awesome car for a low price, but when dealing in high miles as soon as you build it up something may go wrong.  Anyhow, I feel secure that you see where I am coming from, and it was a pleasure bantering about it.<P>Seth<BR>85 GT 3.1 4.10 ||||reg||1
